A `GlobalFilter` for JWT validation inspects the `Authorization` header before forwarding. If the token is missing or invalid, it short-circuits the filter chain and returns 401 immediately — the downstream service never receives the unauthorized request.

```java
@Component
public class JwtAuthFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        String token = exchange.getRequest()
            .getHeaders().getFirst(HttpHeaders.AUTHORIZATION);

        if (token == null || !isValidJwt(token)) {
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete(); // stops the chain
        }

        // Add extracted claims as headers for downstream services
        ServerHttpRequest mutated = exchange.getRequest().mutate()
            .header("X-User-Id", extractUserId(token))
            .build();
        return chain.filter(exchange.mutate().request(mutated).build());
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() { return -1; } // run before all other filters
}
```

`isValidJwt()` must be **non-blocking**. JWT signature verification (using a public key cached in memory) is CPU-only — non-blocking by nature. If you instead validate by calling an auth service via JDBC or a synchronous `RestTemplate`, you block the Netty event-loop thread. That thread handles I/O for hundreds of concurrent connections. One blocking call per request means the event loop stalls, connection queues grow, and throughput collapses.

The fix for any I/O-dependent validation: return a `Mono` from the validation step and use reactive operators (`flatMap`, `switchIfEmpty`) to chain the filter logic. The Gateway stays non-blocking end-to-end, and the event loop thread is never held.

In OPEN state, Resilience4j rejects every call immediately with `CallNotPermittedException`. No downstream call is made. Threads are freed instantly rather than blocked for seconds waiting for a timeout. The fallback method runs instead, returning a degraded but valid response — cached data, empty list, service-specific default — that the caller can work with.

After `waitDurationInOpenState` (typically 30–60 seconds), the circuit transitions automatically to **HALF_OPEN**. It doesn't close based on a timer — it *probes*. `permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState` (default: 10) determines how many test calls are allowed through to the downstream service.

If those test calls pass the failure and slow-call thresholds, the circuit closes and full traffic resumes. If they fail, the circuit reopens and the wait cycle starts again.

The probe-before-close design prevents the **thundering herd problem.** If a circuit opens during a traffic spike and then closes immediately, the full traffic surge hits the just-recovered downstream service simultaneously — potentially re-failing it. HALF_OPEN allows only `permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState` test calls, giving the downstream service a chance to demonstrate stable recovery before absorbing full load.

The fallback method is the key UX decision. It must have the same return type as the protected method, plus a `Throwable` parameter. It receives both `CallNotPermittedException` (circuit OPEN) and actual downstream exceptions (circuit CLOSED but call failed), so it must handle both cases gracefully.

```java
@CircuitBreaker(name = "orderService", fallbackMethod = "fallbackOrder")
public Order getOrder(String orderId) {
    return orderClient.fetchOrder(orderId);
}

private Order fallbackOrder(String orderId, Throwable t) {
    // Return a cached or default value — never propagate the error to the caller
    return orderCache.getOrDefault(orderId, Order.empty());
}
```

The common mistake from the interviewer's perspective: COUNT_BASED with `slidingWindowSize(5)` and `failureRateThreshold(50)`. In a service that handles 2 requests per minute, that 5-call window spans 2.5 minutes. Three failures in a slow afternoon of low traffic trips the circuit — on what might be normal variance. The circuit stays OPEN through `waitDurationInOpenState`, rejecting calls while the downstream service is perfectly healthy. Users see errors; engineers investigate; nothing is wrong.

**TIME_BASED** windows give a statistically meaningful sample that scales with traffic. High-traffic services get thousands of data points in 10 seconds; low-traffic services get the few calls that actually occurred. The circuit doesn't trip on 3 bad calls in a 5-call window; it trips when a genuine pattern of failures emerges over time.

For integration testing, Resilience4j provides `CircuitBreakerRegistry` with forced state transition methods — you don't need to manufacture the exact number of failures to trigger a transition:

```java
@Autowired CircuitBreakerRegistry circuitBreakerRegistry;

@Test
void fallback_returnsDefault_whenCircuitIsOpen() {
    CircuitBreaker cb = circuitBreakerRegistry.circuitBreaker("orderService");
    cb.transitionToOpenState(); // force OPEN — no need to trigger N failures

    Order result = orderService.getOrder("order-123");

    assertThat(result).isEqualTo(Order.empty()); // fallback executed
    cb.transitionToClosedState(); // restore for subsequent tests
}
```

This makes circuit breaker tests deterministic and fast. No need to mock exactly N failures, configure thresholds to trip on a single call, or sleep waiting for window evaluation. Force the state, test the behavior, restore the state. This is the testing pattern that demonstrates real production experience with Resilience4j.

The `@LoadBalanced` annotation must go on the **bean definition method**, not the injection point or call site. This is the configuration mistake that produces `UnknownHostException: order-service` with no obvious cause.

```java
@Bean
@LoadBalanced  // registers LoadBalancerInterceptor on this specific RestTemplate
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    return new RestTemplate();
}

// This works — uses the @LoadBalanced bean
restTemplate.getForObject("http://order-service/api/orders", List.class);

// This fails — new RestTemplate has no interceptor
new RestTemplate().getForObject("http://order-service/api/orders", List.class);
```

For the cache staleness problem during rapid deployments: `spring.cloud.loadbalancer.cache.ttl=10s` reduces the refresh interval to 10 seconds. This narrows the window where terminated instances receive traffic during rolling deployments. Set it too low and you increase load on the service registry; 10–15 seconds is usually a good balance.

**Zone-aware routing** is the to_stand_out configuration for multi-AZ cloud deployments. Setting `spring.cloud.loadbalancer.zone=us-east-1a` on each instance causes the load balancer to prefer instances in the same availability zone. Cross-AZ calls add ~5–10ms latency and incur data transfer costs at scale. The load balancer falls back to other zones when local instances are unavailable, so availability isn't sacrificed for latency.

For **canary routing** or A/B testing, implement a custom `ServiceInstanceListSupplier`. The supplier receives the full list of available instances and returns a filtered or weighted subset. A supplier that reads a `version` tag from instance metadata can route 10% of traffic to `version=2.0` instances — canary deployment without any proxy configuration changes.

**Never trace 100% of requests in production.** Tracing adds serialization, network traffic to the collector, and storage cost. A high-traffic service at 10,000 req/s generates 10,000 spans per second at 100% sampling — overwhelming Zipkin's storage. `probability: 0.1` (10%) captures enough traffic to understand behavior without the cost.

The gap in probabilistic sampling: low-frequency errors. A 0.1% error rate at 10% sampling means 1 in 1000 error requests is traced. For debugging production incidents, that's often insufficient. Implement a custom `Sampler` that forces sampling for any request that results in a 5xx response:

```java
@Bean
Sampler errorForcingSampler() {
    return (traceContext, span) -> {
        // Force sampling for error spans regardless of global probability
        if (span.tags().containsKey("error")) return SamplingFlags.SAMPLED;
        return SamplingFlags.NOT_SAMPLED_OR_PARENT; // use global rate
    };
}
```

**Custom spans** add business context that auto-instrumentation cannot provide. Knowing a payment processing span took 4 seconds is useful. Knowing it was processing a $50,000 transaction is more useful for prioritization.

```java
@Autowired Tracer tracer;

Span span = tracer.nextSpan().name("process-payment").start();
try (Tracer.SpanInScope scope = tracer.withSpan(span)) {
    span.tag("payment.amount", amount.toString());
    span.tag("payment.currency", currency);
    span.tag("payment.method", paymentMethod);
    return paymentGateway.charge(amount, currency);
} catch (Exception e) {
    span.error(e);
    throw e;
} finally {
    span.end();
}
```

With `payment.amount` as a span tag in Zipkin, you can filter: "show me all traces where payment amount > $10,000 that took longer than 3 seconds." This is observability with business context — the difference between "something was slow" and "high-value transactions are slow."

**Choose Feign** for standard service-to-service REST calls in a Spring MVC application. The interface-based model makes the client contract explicit, readable, and easy to mock in tests. The Spring Cloud integration is the decisive advantage — add `spring-cloud-starter-openfeign`, `spring-cloud-starter-loadbalancer`, and `spring-cloud-starter-circuitbreaker-resilience4j` to the classpath, and Feign automatically gains load balancing, circuit breaking, and distributed trace propagation.

```java
@FeignClient(name = "order-service", fallback = OrderFallback.class)
public interface OrderClient {
    @GetMapping("/api/orders/{id}")
    Order getOrder(@PathVariable("id") String orderId);

    @PostMapping("/api/orders")
    Order createOrder(@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest request);
}
```

This interface is the complete client implementation. No boilerplate. Load balancing resolves `order-service` to a live instance. A circuit breaker wraps every call. Trace IDs propagate in headers automatically.

**Choose WebClient** when your application is on Spring WebFlux, when you need non-blocking concurrent fan-out (`Mono.zip()` to call three services simultaneously without blocking three threads), or when consuming streaming responses like Server-Sent Events.

**Choose RestClient** (Spring Boot 3.2+) for new synchronous services that want a modern, fluent API without reactive complexity and without Feign's Spring Cloud dependency chain. It's the synchronous HTTP client Spring intends to replace RestTemplate going forward.

**Don't choose RestTemplate** for new code. When you encounter it in existing codebases, migrate it to Feign or RestClient as you touch those areas — not as a separate refactoring effort, but incrementally.
